      "content": "it's just so unsettling how much they're playing with these \"rhythms\" and \"subconscious responses\" without being transparent about it. if they're using mathematically precise geometry specifically to trigger these impulsive responses, aren't they basically using a form of neuromarketing that's designed to override our ability to make free choices? \n\nit feels predatory. like, if you can map out the exact geometric intersections that force a limbic response, isn't that basically a psychological hack? can you look into whether there's any precedent for this kind of \"hardcoded\" visual stimulus being used to manipulate consumer behavior on a neurological level?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
